description:
Workspace-wide propagation of the JMAP-h47t.22 fix from tasks-server to the canonical jmap-mail-server and the remaining 6 extension-server siblings.

The workspace AGENTS.md 'Caller identity (foundation seam)' section establishes the rule that backends are canonical for permission enforcement and handlers do NO permission checking. Per-crate AGENTS.md repeats this. But docs.rs visitors do not read AGENTS.md — they read the trait method rustdoc, and currently NONE of the extension *Backend mutating methods (create_object, update_object, destroy_object, plus extension-specific writers like copy_email, slow_mode_check, apply_space_patch, etc.) mention the auth-enforcement contract in their docstring.

Failure mode: a consumer implements *Backend as a thin wrapper around their storage layer, registers the handlers, and ships — with NO authorisation check anywhere in their JMAP path. The handler doesn't check (correctly, per workspace rule), and the backend doesn't check (because the consumer didn't know they had to). Result: anyone with a valid HTTP path through the dispatcher can mutate any account.

Scope:
  jmap-mail-server   MailBackend (canonical) — create_object, update_object, destroy_object,
                                              copy_email, plus per-method extras (parse_email,
                                              send_submission, etc.)
  jmap-chat-server   ChatBackend — create_object, update_object, destroy_object,
                                              apply_space_patch, generate_invite_code,
                                              slow_mode_check, is_contact_blocked,
                                              may_set_custom_emoji, expire_message, retains_edit_history
  jmap-calendars-server CalendarsBackend — create_object, update_object, destroy_object,
                                              copy_calendar_event, get_principal_default_calendar
  jmap-contacts-server ContactsBackend — create_object, update_object, destroy_object
  jmap-filenode-server FileNodeBackend — create_object, update_object, destroy_object, copy_filenode
  jmap-metadata-server MetadataBackend — create_object, update_object, destroy_object
  jmap-sharing-server  SharingBackend — set_principal_acl, etc.

Pattern (copy from tasks-server backend.rs):

    /// # Authorisation contract
    ///
    /// This method is the canonical enforcement point for caller authorisation
    /// per workspace AGENTS.md 'Caller identity (foundation seam)' and per-crate
    /// AGENTS.md 'Permission enforcement: backend canonical'. Implementors MUST
    /// verify that the principal identified by JmapBackend::principal_id(caller)
    /// has write access to the target before performing the mutation, and MUST
    /// return BackendSetError::SetError(SetErrorType::Forbidden) otherwise.
    /// Handlers in this crate do NO permission checking; any handler-layer
    /// pre-check is defense-in-depth only and the backend MUST re-verify
    /// atomically with the mutation. A backend that trusts the handler is
    /// a bug; a backend that returns None from principal_id is signalling
    /// 'single-user posture' and CANNOT correctly implement ACLs.

For methods after the first on each trait, the shorter form is fine:

    /// # Authorisation contract
    ///
    /// Implementors MUST verify caller write-access to the target before the
    /// mutation and MUST return BackendSetError::SetError(Forbidden) otherwise.
    /// See [`Self::create_object`] for the workspace-canonical rationale.

Read-side probes (task_list_has_tasks-style) get a different shorter form noting that the surrounding /set flow re-validates and that per-principal filtering avoids existence leaks.

Canonical-first ordering: change jmap-mail-server's MailBackend trait first, then the 6 siblings in one pass each. This sweep is docstring-only — no behavior change, no signature change — and is mechanical once the canonical wording is settled. Inversion note: tasks-server got the rustdoc first (JMAP-h47t.22 was scoped to that crate); when this sweep lands the canonical, the tasks-server wording should match — minor reconciliation may be needed if the canonical version diverges in phrasing.
